  "text": "umably, the equation in base $b$ can be expressed as:\n$$A \\cdot b^3 + B \\cdot b^2 + C \\cdot b + D + B \\cdot b^3 + C \\cdot b^2 + D \\cdot b + A = E \\cdot b^3 + F \\cdot b^2 + G \\cdot b + H + I \\cdot b^3 + J \\cdot b^2 + K \\cdot b + L$$\n\nSimplifying the left side gives:\n$$2A \\cdot b^3 + 2B \\cdot b^2 + 2C \\cdot b + 2D + A = E \\cdot b^3 + F \\cdot b^2 + G \\cdot b + H + I \\cdot b^3 + J \\cdot b^2 + K \\cdot b + L$$\n\nSince the equation holds true for all digits $A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, L$, we can equate the coefficients of $b^3, b^2, b, 1$ on both sides:\n$$2A = E + I$$\n$$2B = F + J$$\n$$2C = G + K$$\n$$2D = H + L$$\n\nFrom these equations, we can deduce that $A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, L$ are all different digits in base $b$. Since $A$ is the first digit, it must be $1$.\n\nIf $b > 10$, then $A = 1$ would mean that $b$ is at least $10$, but then $E$ and $I$ would be $10$ and $11$ or higher, which is not possible in base $b$ since the digits are from $0$ to $b-1$.\n\nTherefore, $b$ must be $10$.",
